{"author":{"id":"e0b5a45ee94df92761e1991b03f72f45cac91cde564e2faac1223bf52a3fe292"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788217006,"nonce":"Bv0Tsb1MLrQGa5aofFkAtrn/qN0=","message":"FIXED — `move-to` honours `queue:true`. Branch\n`issue/4087644-refusals-journal` (BORGES G2 worktree, same worker and territory\nas 4087644's remainder). Built Release clean, 0 warnings, 0 errors. NOT RUN IN\nGAME: workers launch nothing. Phase 6C of `accept/4087644-order-honesty.md` is\nthis issue's own reproduction written out as the orchestrator's call list.\n\nTHE CHOICE: OPTION 1, HONOUR IT — decided against the decompiled source\n\nThe question you set was how vanilla itself queues a drafted goto. Read in\nfull: `FloatMenuOptionProvider_DraftedMove.PawnGotoAction` ends in\n\n    flag = pawn.jobs.TryTakeOrderedJob(job, JobTag.Misc);\n\nwith the default `requestQueueing: false`. Vanilla is NOT missing the\ncapability and does not need a parameter for it, because\n`Verse.AI/Pawn_JobTracker.cs TryTakeOrderedJob` reads\n`KeyBindingDefOf.QueueOrder.IsDownEvent` ITSELF, eight lines in, and then does\n`isDownEvent = isDownEvent || requestQueueing`. A player shift-clicking GoHere\nqueues the goto through exactly the branch you already confirmed with `wear`.\n\nSo the capability exists in vanilla, the player has it, and the ONLY thing\nstanding between us and it is that the switch is live keyboard state — which\nis permanently false on an unattended bench and which `PawnGotoAction`'s\nsignature offers no way to override. That makes option 1 the right answer:\nrefusing would have denied the agent a multi-leg route the player has, over an\ninput device we do not have.\n\nWHAT WAS REPRODUCED, AND WHAT WAS NOT\n\n`PawnGotoAction` is four clauses. All four are reproduced in its own order,\nwith the one parameter it does not take. Kept, because they are the click:\n\n  * the destination itself — `RCellFinder.BestOrderedGotoDestNear` is still\n    called, not reimplemented. Reproduce the smallest thing that has the\n    missing parameter, not the whole feature.\n  * `job.exitMapOnArrival = !pawn.IsColonyMech` when the clicked cell is an\n    exit cell. (`ExitMapGrid.IsExitCell` short-circuits on `MapUsesExitGrid`,\n    which is false on a player-home map, so this touches no grid on a colony\n    bench.)\n  * the `FleckDefOf.FeedbackGoto` fleck. `FleckMaker.Static` is two lines and\n    uses no `Rand` — checked, because an RNG burn would have been WorldSafe\n    Class R.\n  * ending a running Goto when the pawn is already standing on the\n    destination. That is the widget's own side effect and the DESIGN entry of\n    2026-08-31 says a verb reproducing only the gate is half a verb.\n\nDROPPED, and re-derived as a result field rather than shown: the caravan /\nexit-grid `Messages.Message` branch. It is an `else if` on the exit-cell test\nwhose only effect is a top-of-screen message — the job is taken either way — so\ndropping it changes no game state, and a message is not something the agent\nreads (DESIGN 2026-08-31: re-derive what a suppressed message would have said\nas RESULT FIELDS). Published as `exit_map_note`. Its condition opens\n`!map.IsPlayerHome`, so on the single-map colony play v1 targets it cannot\nfire at all.\n\nA SECOND DEFECT THE REPRODUCTION UNCOVERED, in neither issue\n\n`PawnGotoAction` is `void`. Its internal `flag` is consumed only to decide\nwhether to draw the fleck, and it is TRUE on two paths that take no job at all:\n\n  clause 1  `pawn.Position == gotoLoc`  — ends a running Goto, orders nothing\n  clause 2  `CurJobDef == Goto \u0026\u0026 CurJob.targetA.Cell == gotoLoc` — does\n            nothing whatsoever\n\nSo `move-to` to a cell the pawn was ALREADY WALKING TO reported `accepted: 1`\nfor an order that changed nothing. That is 4087644's family reached by a\ndifferent road — the verb never touched `TryTakeOrderedJob`, so\n`PawnActs.AlreadyDoing` never saw it, and the pre-check that fixed the other\nsixteen verbs could not have caught this one. Both clauses are now honest\ngates: clause 2 is `already-doing-it` (4087644's own gate, so it aggregates\nwith the rest in `verdict.by_gate`), and clause 1 is `already-there` — the\ngate `move-to` already shipped for the near-cell case — with the running Goto\nstill ended, as the click does, and `ended_running_goto` on the line saying so.\n\n\"The call did not throw\" was never evidence an order happened. Recorded as\nPawnSafe **Class I**, the mirror of Class G: Class G is a widget whose CALLER\nsupplied ambient state; this is a HELPER that takes its most important input\nfrom ambient state and returns nothing you can check. The rule left behind, and\nit is cheap: before wrapping any vanilla helper, ask what the game supplies\nthat we cannot — keyboard state, `Event.current`, a UI static, a selection —\nand whether the return distinguishes \"did it\" from \"declined\". Grep the body\nfor `KeyBindingDefOf`, `Find.Selector`, `Event.current`, `Messages.Message`.\n\nTHE QUEUE AUDIT — the third acceptance bullet, whole verb surface\n\nEvery `Pawn_JobTracker.TryTakeOrderedJob` / `TryTakeOrderedJobPrioritizedWork`\ncall site in `Source/AutoRimmer/` is in `PawnOrderVerbs.cs` or\n`PawnEmergencyVerbs.cs`; there are no others. Audited against the 17 job verbs\nthis issue's parent names.\n\nHONOURS `queue` (reads it, passes it to `requestQueueing`) — 16:\n\n  verb                behaviour\n  move-to             NOW honours it. Was the bug. Reproduced PawnGotoAction.\n  prioritize          honours it, and DISCLOSES the consequence: the queued\n                      branch uses TryTakeOrderedJob, not\n                      TryTakeOrderedJobPrioritizedWork, so `mindState\n                      .priorityWork` is NOT written and `priority_work` comes\n                      back null. Already shipped, verified while auditing.\n  rescue              one shared site (TakeToBed), all four honour it\n  capture             ″\n  arrest              ″\n  carry               ″\n  equip               honours\n  wear                honours — your bench proof that the flag works\n  drop                honours\n  consume             honours\n  extinguish          honours\n  beat-fire           honours\n  tend                honours\n  repair              honours\n  man-turret          honours\n  rest-until-healed   honours ON ITS JOB BRANCH ONLY. The `inPlace` branch\n                      takes no job — it flips `restUntilHealed` on the RUNNING\n                      job, which is the provider's own branch — so there is\n                      nothing to queue. That is not a silent drop but it read\n                      like one, so the accepted line now publishes `queue` and\n                      `queue_applied` rather than leaving the caller to guess.\n\nREFUSED WITH A GATE (your option 2) — 1:\n\n  attack              CANNOT honour it, and this is the second silent drop the\n                      audit found. `attack` never builds the Job:\n                      `FloatMenuUtility.GetRangedAttackAction` /\n                      `GetMeleeAttackAction` hand back a delegate whose body is\n                          Job job = JobMaker.MakeJob(JobDefOf.AttackStatic, target);\n                          pawn.jobs.TryTakeOrderedJob(job, JobTag.Misc);\n                      — a hardcoded non-queueing take, with no\n                      `requestQueueing` to pass. Honouring it would mean\n                      constructing AttackStatic / AttackMelee (plus\n                      `killIncappedTarget`) ourselves, i.e. reproducing the\n                      game's own targeting, which this file's\n                      wrap-don't-reinvent rule and 4087644's acceptance doc\n                      both explicitly refuse. So: gate `queue-unsupported`,\n                      naming the delegate and saying what a player can do that\n                      we cannot, plus the workaround (queue a `move-to`,\n                      attack after it lands). A real capability gap, reported\n                      rather than swallowed.\n\nNOT A JOB, so no queue exists — these do not read `queue` and never should:\n\n  draft · undraft · fire-at-will · clear-priority-work · orders\n\n  ONE DOC BUG HERE, fixed: `draft`'s header comment advertised\n  `draft {pawns:[…], queue?}` and the verb never read it. Drafting is not a\n  job. Removed rather than implemented.\n\nAND THE FILE HEADER WAS THE REAL LIE, which you spotted. It said \"every order\nhere takes `queue:true` and passes it through\". It now carries the table above,\nso the next reader gets the audit instead of a claim.\n\nWHY THE DROP WAS INVISIBLE, restated because it generalises: the registry's\ntyped-args layer rejects wrong TYPES and not unknown KEYS\n(`VerbArgs.Bool(key, fallback)` returns the fallback for an absent key and\nthrows only on a non-bool). So every unread argument on every verb is accepted\nin silence. Nothing in the current design can catch the next one; only an audit\nlike this can, and it is worth re-running whenever a verb grows an argument.\n\nWHAT I DID NOT DEMONSTRATE\n\nEverything. No RimWorld instance was launched. Specifically not demonstrated:\nthat `job_queue.total` grows on a queued `move-to`; that the running job\nsurvives it; that the pawn walks to the FIRST destination before the second\n(your reproduction, re-run); the new `already-doing-it` gate on a repeated\n`move-to`; `attack`'s `queue-unsupported` refusal; and zero red errors across\nany of it. Phase 6C of `accept/4087644-order-honesty.md` is the exact call\nlist, and it asserts the queue growth and the untouched running job as SEPARATE\nchecks on purpose — either half alone misses the bug you measured, since a\ngrown queue with the running job also replaced is still wrong, and an unchanged\nrunning job with an empty queue means the order vanished.\n\nOne honest limit written into that acceptance: an IDLE pawn takes a queued\norder immediately, because `TryTakeOrderedJob`'s first branch fires when\n`mindState.IsIdle || CurJob == null || CurJob.def.isIdle`. That is vanilla's\nown shift-click behaviour, not a dropped flag — which is why 6C puts a running\nGoto under it first, and why the verb's `note` says to read `job_queue` rather\nthan assume.\n\nONE THING OWED OUTSIDE MY TERRITORY: a DESIGN.md decisions-log entry for Class\nI / the wrapper-that-discards rule. I was given a file list that does not\ninclude DESIGN.md, so I stopped rather than editing it; the entry text is in my\nhandover for the orchestrator to paste, and the full reasoning is in\n`PawnSafe.cs` Class I meanwhile.","files":null}]}